course: dictionary
lesson: 12050
title: Cite your sources
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When you write a project, you must show where your facts came from. To cite something is to name it clearly, so your reader can check it. «Please cite your sources at the end of your project.» Sources are the books, websites or people you got your information from.
explain: Cite also means to mention something as an example or a reason. «In the debate, Maya cited a study about school lunches to support her point.» Maya did not just say her idea. She named evidence that backed it up. Evidence is proof.
explain: In a court case, cite has a different meaning: to name someone officially. «The lawyer cited the witness by name in court.» It is the same word, but now it is a formal, official act. Look at the words around cite to see which meaning fits.
fact: Cite is a verb: cite, cites, cited, citing.
fact: «Cite» means to name something as an example, a reason or a source.
fact: «Cited» is the past tense: «She cited three books in her project.»
fact: In law, «cite» means to name someone officially in a court case.
fact: A quick check: if «mention» or «name» fits in the sentence and still makes sense, cite probably means something similar.
exercise: «Always cite the websites you used for your research.» What does cite mean here? | *To name where you got your information | To delete the website | To paint a picture of the website | To hide it from your teacher
exercise: Ben says, «He cited the rain as the reason the match was cancelled.» What does cited mean? | Washed away | *Named as the reason | Forgot completely | Sang loudly
exercise: Which word is the past tense of cite? | citing | cite | *cited | cites
test: «The lawyer cited the witness in court.» In this sentence, cite means: | *Named officially in a court case | Painted in bright colours | Ran away quickly | Hid from the judge
test: Mia had to ___ two books in her project. Which word fits the gap? | cube | cute | cycle | *cite
exam: «In your debate, you must cite evidence to support your point.» What does cite mean here? | Forget | *Mention as proof | Shout | Draw
